Z||000000||2FAST4U||02-06-2000||09:08 PM||||It's so good to be back among the Fierofiles of the world.<BR>Getting my new DSL line set up and changing my ISP and email accounts turned out to be more than I thought but I guess it was worth it. This DSL stuff is fast.<BR>I anyone has sent me email in the last week I didn't get it. so please resend to my new email address.<P>jlecroix@uswest.net<P>Since my last post the 85 GT has had every suspension piece and every brake component replaced with new parts, new tires and 16 inch wheels, new custom designed intake tubes and air cleaner, new dog bone and cradle bushings, and a new hand built Stainless exhaust system. It went in the paint shop last Wednesday for Solid Blavk paint. And when it comes out of the paint shop it has an appointment at the upholstry shop for an new interior. Pics in about a month I hope.||||reg||10
Z||000001||superXmotorsports||02-08-2000||06:06 PM||||And a nice GT wing on the back too sometime soon!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Joe is a very nice and patient guy.  What type of custom intake did you fabricate?  SXM.||||reg||1
Z||000002||2FAST4U||02-08-2000||11:47 PM||||I used thin wall aluminum plate to form a closed shell 13 1/2 inches in diameter to house an 11" X 3" K&N airfilter element. This shell sits on it's side where the old air cleaner was. I used aluminum tubing to form a tube from the side scoop to the center of the air cleaner shell and from the side of the air cleaner to the throttle body. I polished all of the aluminum and used neoprene hose and gator clanps to tie it all together. It looks pretty good. I'll post photos when it comes back from the body shop.||||reg||1
